		{
			"id": "116049824775605017",
			"parentId": "116047318571103522",
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@krinkle/116049824734351113",
			"author": {
				"name": "Timo Tijhof",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@krinkle",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/292/532/015/873/530/original/b119a0914bbd5722.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2026-02-11T03:16:36.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>Should be a lot more! They don't organise frontend and npm vuln that way. This doesn't even mention JavaScript:</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-3573\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://www.</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-</span><span class=\"invisible\">3573</span></a></p><p>The search relies on descriptions for which standard terms are \"an ongoing area of research\" 🧐 </p><p><a href=\"https://www.cve.org/ResourcesSupport/FAQs#pc_cve_list_basicssearch_cve\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://www.</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">cve.org/ResourcesSupport/FAQs#</span><span class=\"invisible\">pc_cve_list_basicssearch_cve</span></a></p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "116049876808025362",
			"parentId": "116049824775605017",
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@krinkle/116049876767593449",
			"author": {
				"name": "Timo Tijhof",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@krinkle",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/292/532/015/873/530/original/b119a0914bbd5722.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2026-02-11T03:29:50.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span></p><p>Eco diff is quite distorted.</p><p>CVE dedupes npm such that A using outdated B isn't its own vul, despite requiring a separate upstream fix, and npm pkgs often having deep and wide dep trees (1000+ is common), and npm installing multiple versions, thus end-users don't update just once. You wait to apply each.</p><p>WP pgks are global so a thing only updates once (like Linux). Most have 0 deps, some 1. The deps that exist commit to pubic API forever stable, so direct upgrade is not constrained.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
